      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what that means and why it is unique:\n\n### 1. What it is made of\nWater is composed of two simple elements:\n*   **Hydrogen:** A gas that is the lightest element.\n*   **Oxygen:** A gas that we need to breathe.\n\nTwo atoms of hydrogen are chemically bonded to one atom of oxygen to form a single water molecule.\n\n### 2. Its Physical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means the atoms are arranged in a bent shape, and the electrons are not shared equally. The oxygen atom pulls the electrons toward itself, giving it a slight negative charge, while the hydrogen atoms have a slight positive charge.\n\nThis polarity is what makes water so special. It allows water molecules to stick to one another (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water can form droplets and flow.\n\n### 3. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, the molecules move slowly and lock into a rigid crystal lattice. Interestingly, ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking and washing. It is the only state that can cover the Earth's surface in large amounts.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Water Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air.\n\n### 4. Why it is essential\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. It is the foundation of life on Earth because:\n*   It acts as a medium for chemical reactions inside cells.\n*   It regulates body temperature (sweating and panting).\n*   It transports nutrients and oxygen to cells.\n*   It lubricates joints and protects tissues and organs."
